---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Research — LUNA"
  description="Research notes, observations, experiments, architecture questions, and public artifacts from the LUNA longitudinal cognitive architecture project."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">LUNA RESEARCH</p>
      <h1>Evidence before mythology.</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Versioned observations, architectural questions, controlled experiments, and
        falsifiable predictions about persistent, history-dependent artificial cognition.
      </p>
      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>Publication discipline:</strong> observations, interpretations, accepted architecture, and implemented capabilities are kept distinct. Internal notes are rewritten for public release rather than published as reconstruction guides.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="research-category-grid">
        <div class="research-category-card"><h3>Early LUNA Experiences</h3><p>Observed interactions preserved as prospective baselines, with explicit attention to what they show and what they do not.</p></div>
        <div class="research-category-card"><h3>Architecture Notes</h3><p>Public conceptual explanations of mechanisms such as associative cognition, developmental plasticity, perception, and continuity.</p></div>
        <div class="research-category-card"><h3>ChronoForge Research</h3><p>Longitudinal simulation, branching developmental trajectories, resulting histories, developmental timing, participant comparison, and controlled causal experiments.</p></div>
        <div class="research-category-card"><h3>Experiments & Results</h3><p>Measured comparisons and case studies as the relevant systems become testable. Negative results belong here too.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="research-section-title">
        <h2>Early LUNA Experiences</h2>
        <span>Observed artifacts from the running system</span>
      </div>
      <div class="research-list">
        <a class="research-item" href="/research/when-memory-is-not-enough">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />LUNA Core v0.5</div>
          <div>
            <h2>When Memory Is Not Enough: Can an AI Be Changed by Experience?</h2>
            <p>An accidental control condition on storage, retrieval, autobiographical creation, causal use, and developmental integration.</p>
            <span class="tag">Early LUNA Experience III</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>

        <a class="research-item" href="/research/purpose-and-self-interpretation">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />LUNA Core v0.5</div>
          <div>
            <h2>Purpose, Self-Interpretation, and Learning From Experience</h2>
            <p>LUNA forms a self-referential interpretation from remembered architecture, while the memory graph refuses to immediately canonize it as identity.</p>
            <span class="tag">Early LUNA Experience II</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>

        <a class="research-item" href="/research/luna-describes-luna">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />LUNA v0.5</div>
          <div>
            <h2>LUNA Describes LUNA</h2>
            <p>A self-description artifact: how the running system organized the Stack/Sleeve distinction, persistent runtime, memory, and developmental aims when asked what makes LUNA different.</p>
            <span class="tag">Self-description artifact</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="research-section-title">
        <h2>Architecture Notes</h2>
        <span>Public conceptual layer · implementation details protected</span>
      </div>
      <div class="research-list">
        <a class="research-item" href="/research/memory-can-remind-memory">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />Associative cognition</div>
          <div>
            <h2>Memory Can Remind Memory</h2>
            <p>Why retrieval is only the first step: memories and cognitive products can become new cues inside a bounded activation field.</p>
            <span class="tag">Reverie · Cognitive Activation</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>

        <a class="research-item" href="/research/continuous-exposure-not-continuous-thought">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />Perception</div>
          <div>
            <h2>Continuous Exposure Is Not Continuous Thought</h2>
            <p>The red-chair problem: persistent world models, object permanence, habituation, prediction error, and bounded sensory compute.</p>
            <span class="tag">Perception · Environmental continuity</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>

        <a class="research-item" href="/research/remembering-is-not-becoming">
          <div class="meta">August 2026<br />Development</div>
          <div>
            <h2>Remembering Is Not Becoming</h2>
            <p>Why formative experience must be able to leave durable effects without keeping the originating episode permanently salient.</p>
            <span class="tag">Developmental plasticity · Imprinting</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow">→</div>
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="research-section-title">
        <h2>ChronoForge</h2>
        <span>Temporal development and longitudinal evaluation</span>
      </div>
      <div class="artifact-card" style="margin-top:18px">
        <h3>Experience as programmable infrastructure</h3>
        <p>ChronoForge is the project for coherent simulated timelines, delayed consequences, branching developmental trajectories, and controlled comparisons of the histories that emerge across participants and starting checkpoints.</p>
        <div class="page-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/chronoforge">Explore ChronoForge</a></div>
      </div>

      <div class="status-strip">
        <strong>Publication policy</strong>
        <span>Source code, exact prompts, internal schemas, thresholds, weighting systems, detailed causal topology, and simulator-generation mechanics remain private where disclosure would primarily reduce reproduction cost.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
